      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
          "content": "The objective of this study was to compare outcomes of induced labour with intravenous oxytocin with a start in the evening versus in the morning."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "DESIGN",
          "content": "Randomised controlled trial."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "SETTING",
          "content": "Labour wards of three hospitals in Amsterdam, the Netherlands."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "PARTICIPANTS",
          "content": "Women with an indication for induction of labour with intravenous oxytocin."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "Included women were randomized to either the evening group with a start of induction of labour at 21:00 hours, or the morning group with a start at 07:00 hours."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "MAIN OUTCOME MEASURES",
          "content": "Primary outcome was duration of labour. Secondary outcomes were instrumental delivery rate, adverse neonatal outcome defined as an Apgar score below 7 after 5 minutes, number and indications of paediatric consults and neonatal admissions, duration of second stage, number of intrapartum infections and necessity of pain relief."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "We randomised 371 women. Mean duration of labour was not significantly different (primiparae: morning 12 hours and 8 minutes versus evening 11 hours and 22 minutes, P value 0.29; multiparae: morning 7 hours and 34 minutes versus evening 7 hours and 46 minutes, P value 0.70). There were no significant differences in instrumental deliveries rates, number of infections or patient satisfaction. Unexpectedly, neonatal outcome was better in women induced in the evening."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION",
          "content": "Induction of labour with intravenous oxytocin in the evening is equally effective as induction in the morning."
        }
      ]},
